WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.020
A request crosses two very different

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.790
places on its way to your business logic.

3
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.590
Where a policy runs changes what it can do.

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.580
First, the API gateway, at the edge.

5
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.750
Then the last mile

6
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.270
— the pipeline inside each service.

7
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.580
The gateway sees traffic before it fans out.

8
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.700
That makes it perfect for early, coarse, high-leverage policies.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.850
Authentication.

10
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:27.830
Rate limiting.

11
00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.490
Can this caller reach this API family?

12
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:32.280
Request normalization.

13
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.040
Its superpower is leverage:

14
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.810
one config protects everything behind it.

15
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.220
But the gateway has no domain context.

16
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.250
It doesn't know this order belongs to that tenant,

17
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.350
or that a user may edit only their own records.

18
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.230
The last mile runs inside the service,

19
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.470
where the model and the data live.

20
00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.700
That's where context-rich policies belong:

21
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.830
fine-grained authorization, multi-tenancy,

22
00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.510
idempotency, business-aware logging, and i18n.

23
00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.260
Its superpower is context.

24
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.050
But a last-mile-only policy does nothing for

25
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.030
traffic the service should never have received.

26
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.480
Reach is the trade.

27
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.720
Here's the rule of thumb.

28
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.820
Decidable from the request alone?

29
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:14.330
Gateway.

30
00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.530
Needs the resource?

31
00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.420
Last mile.

32
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.410
Real systems do both.

33
00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.930
Authenticate at the edge, to keep junk out cheaply.

34
00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:25.070
Authorize the specific resource at the last mile

35
00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:28.290
— so it's safe even if traffic arrives another way:

36
00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.450
an internal call, a message, a future gateway.

37
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.840
That redundancy is defence in depth

38
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.870
— not waste.

39
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.470
So why don't teams do this cleanly?

40
00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.860
The gateway and the service are usually different technology,

41
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.000
with different programming models.

42
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.810
So you write authorization twice, in two styles

43
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.040
— and they drift.

44
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.710
What if the same policy could run in both places

45
00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.840
— and configuration decided where?

46
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.600
That's the next episode.
